  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $B?$\n\nI. $B=[\\begin{aligned}12 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$",
  "worked_solution": "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $B,$ we write $B=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nB=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because  the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains numbers different from $1.$\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}12 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
